  "summary": "Screened all 52 clients with client_updates, client_performance, drift_by_round, feature_summary and subgroup_outcomes (Sex and AgeGroup). Three clients (GA, MN, NY) show a clear, consistent poisoning signature: their updates are anti-aligned with the federation median (cos about -1) and 10-20 times its norm, in every round. Their local performance and feature statistics look normal, so this is not a data fault. I did not report several weaker leads: PR has a lower update cosine (mean 0.71) but its per-round pattern is erratic with no consistent onset, and its drift and CITL are within the normal range; DC has a z of about -3.7 to -5.6 on update cosine and -3.4 on AUC, below the confirmation threshold; GU, VI and NH show small cosine deviations that are consistent with their small sizes. Feature outliers (AL BMI, CT GenHlth, WV MentHlth, ND PhysHlth, NM/NV Sex) and subgroup outliers (GA, CT, WY in AgeGroup) have |z_noise| of about 3-6 and no second supporting signal, so I did not report them. No evidence of label noise, prevalence shift, drift, subgroup bias, missing-artefact sentinels, free riding or explanation instability was found beyond noise. Budget was fully spent (20.0)."
